   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the Indian law, in relation to the state recognition and\nacknowledgement of the Montaukett Indians",
    "basePrintNo": "S7770",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7770-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §2, add Art 11 §§170 - 173, Indian L",
    "lawSection": "Indian Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7770\n\nSPONSOR: LAVALLE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the Indian law, in relation to the\nstate recognition and acknowledgement of the Montaukett Indians\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo provide State recognition and acknowledgement of the Montaukett Indi-\nans.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Amends section 2 of the Indian law to include the Montaukett\nIndians within the term \"Indian nation or tribe\" in New York.\n\nSection 2. Amends the Indian Law by adding a new Article 11 relating to\nthe State recognition and acknowledgement of the Montaukett Indians.\n\nSection 3. Provides for the effective date of this act.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe Native American Montaukett Tribe has a long, culturally rich history\non the East End of Long Island. The Montaukett Indians had been formally\nrecognized by the State of New York, until their acknowledgement was\nquestionably removed from in 1910 in the land claim case of  \nPHARAOH V.\nBENSON, 69 Misc. Rep. 241 (Supreme, Suffolk Co., 1910) affirmed 164\nApp. Div. 51, affirmed 222 N.Y. 665, when the Montaukett Indians were\ndeclared to be extinct.  This case was subsequently described in a 1994\nState Supreme Court case,  \nBREAKERS MOTEL, INC. V. SUNBEACH MONTAUK TWO,\nINC., as being of \"questionable propriety\".\n\nHistorically, the New York State Legislature has granted recognition or\nacknowledgment to Indian nations and tribes in the State of New York by\nan act of the Legislature. Just as Chapter 31 of the Laws of 1909 and\nChapter 39 of the Laws of 1978 established Article 9 and Article 10 of\nthe Indian Law that include two other eastern Long Island Indian\nNations, the Shinnecock and Poospatuck (Unkechauge), respectively.\n\nIn 2013, the Governor vetoed a measure passed by the Legislature that\nwould have established criteria for considering the request of the\nMontauketts to be acknowledged by the State. Veto  210 of 2013 included\na directive by the Governor to the Department of State (DOS) to move\nforward with an analysis to determine if State recognition of the\nMontaukett Indians is warranted.\n\nFour years later in 2017 and still no determination by DOS in this\nregard, the Legislature again passed legislation to provide State recog-\nnition and acknowledgement of the Montaukett Indians. The Governor went\non to issue another veto on this initiative stating that analysis is\nstill ongoing and directed the Department to \"continue to proactively\ncommunicate with the Montaukett Indian tribe, as well as any interested\nparties, to obtain all relevant information necessary to complete the\nreview in a timely manner.\"\n\nIt has now been well over a century since the courts of New York State\ndeclared that the Montaukett Indians no longer existed. This misdeed has\nbeen perpetuated over all these years despite the fact that the members\nof this sovereign Indian nation have continued to live in our community,\nmaintain their culture, and govern themselves. This legislation adds a\nnew Article 11 relating to the Montaukett Indians that provides for\ntheir State acknowledgement; leadership, elections and terms of office;\nqualification of voters; and qualifications for office.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2017:S.3006/A.2325 - Veto  174 of 2017\n2015-16: S.375A/A.202A;\n2014:S.7619/A.9704\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the State.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect after (a) completion of a review by the\nSecretary of State, as directed by the Governor, pursuant to Veto 210 of\n2013 and Veto 174 of 2017, and (b) submission of an affirmative written\ndetermination from the Secretary of State to the Assembly, Senate and\nGovernor that state recognition and acknowledgement is warranted;\nprovided however, that if the Secretary of State submits a negative\ndetermination on or before January 1, 2019, this act shall be deemed\nrepealed upon January 1, 2019; and further provided that if the Secre-\ntary of State fails to submit any determination on or before January 1,\n2019, this act shall take effect upon January 1, 2019.\n",
